        <title type="html"><![CDATA[docs(css): scrollbar-color overrides ::-webkit-scrollbar via its computed value, and it inherits]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/45246</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/45246"/>
        <updated>2026-08-19T15:07:33.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19518661?u=60818a27a5d585d5f0b738995f05ae2f6dfacbec&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Two related additions about <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code> taking precedence over the<br>
<code class="notranslate">::-webkit-scrollbar-*</code> pseudo-elements.</p>
<p dir="auto">The <code class="notranslate">::-webkit-scrollbar</code> page already carries a note about this. The<br>
<code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code> page never mentions the pseudo-elements at all, so the warning<br>
is only on the page you would read if you already suspected the interaction. In<br>
practice you meet this from the other direction: you set <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code>, your<br>
<code class="notranslate">::-webkit-scrollbar-*</code> rules stop applying, and you go and read<br>
<code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code>.</p>
<p dir="auto">Neither page mentions that <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code> is <strong>inherited</strong>, which is what makes<br>
this hard to diagnose. An element loses its <code class="notranslate">::-webkit-scrollbar-*</code> styling when<br>
an <strong>ancestor</strong> sets <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code>, though the element itself never sets it,<br>
and nothing in the CSS points at the cause. On macOS the fallback is an overlay<br>
scrollbar of zero width, so the scrollbar is not merely unstyled, it is invisible<br>
and occupies no space. That reads as "my CSS did not load" rather than as a<br>
conflict between two ways of styling scrollbars.</p>
<p dir="auto">The spec is explicit that the trigger is the computed value<br>
(<a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-scrollbars/#scrollbar-color" rel="nofollow">CSS Scrollbars Styling Level 1 §2</a>):</p>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto">On any element or pseudo-element where the computed value of <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code><br>
is anything other than the initial <code class="notranslate">auto</code> value, user agents must ignore any<br>
alternative non-standard means for authors to influence the rendering of<br>
scrollbars, such as the <code class="notranslate">::webkit-scrollbar</code> family of pseudo-elements.</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto">with <code class="notranslate">Inherited: yes</code> in its property definition table.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Changes</h3>
<p dir="auto"><strong><code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code></strong> gains a note, since the page had nothing: the pseudo-elements<br>
are ignored when the computed value is not <code class="notranslate">auto</code>, this reaches elements that<br>
inherit the value, <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color: auto</code> restores the pseudo-element styling, and<br>
a link to the existing fallback section.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong><code class="notranslate">::-webkit-scrollbar</code></strong> keeps its note with two adjustments. "have any value other<br>
than <code class="notranslate">auto</code> set" becomes "their <em>computed</em> value is anything other than <code class="notranslate">auto</code>",<br>
because "set" reads as "declared on this element" and the inherited case is the one<br>
that surprises people. The note also now says which of the two properties inherits:<br>
<code class="notranslate">scrollbar-color</code> is <code class="notranslate">Inherited: yes</code> and <code class="notranslate">scrollbar-width</code> is <code class="notranslate">Inherited: no</code>, so<br>
grouping them under one sentence about inheritance would be wrong.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">Found while debugging a site where <code class="notranslate">html { scrollbar-color: ... }</code> silently disabled<br>
every custom scrollbar underneath it. Measured in Chromium 148, changing only that<br>
one property: a scrolling container went from a 0px scrollbar to 16px, and a demo<br>
box from 0px to 17px. It went unnoticed for two days because the symptom is an<br>
absence, and the page whose purpose was demonstrating the custom scrollbar was<br>
itself affected.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Related</h3>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="2134603283" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/32277" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/issues/32277/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/32277">#32277</a> and <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="2136361472" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/32296" data-hovercard-type="pull_request" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/pull/32296/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/32296">#32296</a> added the note on the <code class="notranslate">::-webkit-scrollbar</code> side.</li>
</ul>]]></content>
